    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Palestine, Arkansas got its name in 1870, possibly after a sawmill worker or from biblical references, according to local lore.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/palestine_arkansas",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Rock Island Railroad arrived in the late 19th century, sparking Main Street commerce and shaping Palestine’s early growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        15,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/palestine_arkansas",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Cotton, rice, soybeans, and livestock have long driven Palestine’s economy, with historic cotton ginning exemplified by Burns Bros.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/palestine_arkansas",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Palestine hosts the 1884‑founded Cumberland Presbyterian Church and the 1899 El Cannon Missionary Baptist Church, the latter once served as a school for Black children.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/palestine_arkansas",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Before incorporation, the area was part of the sparsely populated L’Anguille Township, with land deeds from the 1860s often lost or reconstructed.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/palestine_arkansas",
